  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1101 PM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nAn expansive belt of strong mid-upper level northwesterly flow\nhas begun to overspread into the central Plains. The subtle\nripple in the flow, just moving ashore across Washington and\nOregon this evening, will be our high wind maker later Saturday.\nAs this system traverses the central and northern Rockies early\nSaturday morning, and into the High Plains by the afternoon, a \npotent cold front will sweep through. With it, a strong pressure\ngradient will develop and sweep northwest to southeast across\nwestern and central Kansas Saturday mid afternoon into the \nearly evening. Latest REFS and individual members, including the\nHRRR/RRFS, show widespread high wind criteria being met across \nmuch of the forecast area. They are indicating widespread\nnorth/northwesterly 55-65 mph gusts, with some localized 70 \nmph. The area with the highest probabilities of seeing wind \ngusts on the higher end of those ranges will be out along the \nRoute 83 corridor and points west. However, expect nearly all of\nwestern and south-central Kansas to see these strong winds. \nConfidence is high in this occurrence, so preparations to \nsecure loose outdoor items and holiday decorations should be \ncomplete by Saturday morning. Isolated power outages, wind \ndamage, blowing dust, and hazardous driving conditions are also \nall a possibility with this event. Winds will diminish into the \nmid to late Saturday evening as decoupling occurs with nocturnal\ncooling, but continues breezy to windy conditions will continue\ninto the late evening.\n\nOnto the precipitation side of the system: The continued highest\nchance area to see precipitation will be mainly north of Route\n50 Saturday evening into Sunday morning. P-types will primarily\nbe rain, as reflected in the weather grids, but some wet \nsnowflakes mixing in cannot be ruled out across central Kansas \ngiven crashing 850 mb temperatures through Sunday morning. \nHowever, due to the quick nature of the system, marginal surface\ntemperature, and light aspect to the rain or any possible snow,\nprecipitation is expected to have limited impact.\n\nAs we keep harping, persistent cold air has been hard to come \nby so far this late Fall, even behind a northern stream systems.\nThe wake of this system is not really any different. Low \ntemperatures both Sunday and Monday morning will be 5-10 degrees\nabove normal, though the Sunday high temperature looks to stay \na touch below normal. So any \"cooler\" air will be short lived as\nafternoon temperatures in the 50s and 60s will be commonplace \nthrough much of next week. The start of next week is expected to\nbe dry, with no POPs in the forecast until late Wednesday. LREF\ngrand ensemble (ENS/GEFS/GEPS) show a trough with low pressure \nMSLPs clustering near the forecast area. NBM seems to be onboard\nwith the idea of some precipitation as well as 20-40% POPs are \nin the forecast Wednesday-Thursday.",
